The attack chain involved privilege escalation through registry manipulation and credential dumping on both WORKSTATION6 and WORKSTATION5.","findings":[{"technique_id":"T1685.002","name":"Disable or Modify Cloud Log","parent":"Disable or Modify Tools","tactics":["Defense Impairment"],"behavior":"PowerShell disabled script block logging by modifying cached group policy settings in memory to set EnableScriptBlockLogging and EnableScriptBlockInvocationLogging to 0.","slot":0,"reasoning":"The operator used PowerShell to directly modify cached Group Policy settings in memory, specifically disabling script block logging by setting EnableScriptBlockLogging and EnableScriptBlockInvocationLogging to 0. This is defense impairment targeting PowerShell logging, not Windows Event Log or domain-wide GPO modification. T1562.008 covers disabling or modifying PowerShell logging specifically.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E026","field":"ScriptBlockText","quote":"$712DB['ScriptB'+'lockLogging']['EnableScriptB'+'lockLogging']=0;$712DB['ScriptB'+'lockLogging']['EnableScriptBlockInvocationLogging']=0","ok":true},{"event_id":"E026","field":"ScriptBlockText","quote":"$43DE2=[REF].AssEmbly.GeTTYPE('System.Management.Automation.Utils').\"GetFIe`Ld\"('cachedGroupPolicySettings','N'+'onPublic,Static')","ok":true},{"event_id":"E024","field":"CommandLine","quote":"-noP -sta -w 1 -enc","ok":true}],"attempts":1},{"technique_id":"T1003.001","name":"LSASS Memory","parent":"OS Credential Dumping","tactics":["Credential Access"],"behavior":"wsmprovhost.exe and powershell.exe connected to the LSASS named pipe to extract credentials from the Local Security Authority Subsystem.","slot":1,"reasoning":"Both wsmprovhost.exe and powershell.exe connected directly to the \\lsass named pipe, which is the mechanism for accessing LSASS process memory to extract credentials. This matches T1003.001 (LSASS Memory dumping) rather than registry-based alternatives like LSA Secrets or SAM, since the evidence shows pipe conn